<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Vytautas the Great</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1350–<a href="/years/1430">1430</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Vytautas the Great of Lithuania, Witold · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminids">Gediminids</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Vytautas the Great was Grand Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> from 1392 to 1430, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kestutis">Kęstutis</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trakai">Trakai</a> and cousin of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>. He led Lithuanian forces with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a> to victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Knights</a> at Grunwald in 1410, extended the grand duchy towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and settled Karaites and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> at Trakai.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vytautas-the-great.jpg" alt="Vytautas the Great" width="320" height="330" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seal_of_Vytautas_the_Great.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Vytautas was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kestutis">Kęstutis</a> and his wife Birutė and a cousin and childhood friend of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a>. His uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> and his father Kęstutis had ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> jointly, Algirdas the east and Kęstutis the west, which was chiefly responsible for defence against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>. After Algirdas died in 1377 and Jogaila succeeded him, a struggle for power followed. Kęstutis seized <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> in 1381 but was arrested with Vytautas and found dead a week later, and whether he died naturally or was murdered is still debated. Vytautas escaped in 1382, sought help from the Teutonic Order and for years switched between the Order and Jogaila.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-krewo">Union of Krewo</a> in 1385 made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/w-adys-aw-ii-jagie-o">Jogaila</a> king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, Vytautas attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a> in 1389 and allied again with the Order. In 1392 Jogaila offered to make him regent in place of the unpopular Skirgaila. In the Astrava Treaty Vytautas recovered all his father's lands, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trakai">Trakai</a>, and in practice held all the Grand Duke's power. The Union of Vilnius and Radom in 1401 recognised him as Grand Duke for his lifetime, with Jogaila as Supreme Duke, and with the title to pass to the Polish king at his death.</p><p>He sought to control Ruthenian lands and helped the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>'s khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a>. In 1398 his army attacked part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, so that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> spanned the Baltic to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, and he brought Karaim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> families, 388 of them Karaim, to guard castles and bridges. A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crusade">crusade</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> ended in crushing defeat at the Vorskla River in 1399. In 1410 he commanded the forces of the Grand Duchy at Grunwald, a decisive Polish-Lithuanian victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>By the Peace of Thorn of 1411 Vytautas received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samogitia">Samogitia</a> for his lifetime, and it was returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in perpetuity by the Treaty of Lake Melno after a war in 1422. He helped create the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-horodlo">Union of Horodło</a> of 1413, which gave Lithuanian Christian nobles the same rights as the Polish szlachta. At the Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutsk">Lutsk</a> in January 1429 Sigismund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a> proposed that Vytautas be crowned King of Lithuania, which caused a crisis with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Vytautas died at Trakai Island Castle on 27 October 1430, ending the affair, and was buried in the Cathedral of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>. In modern Lithuania he is revered as a national hero, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vytautas-magnus-university">Vytautas Magnus University</a> is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Vytautas the Great” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Even so, the emir Edigu crushed <b>Vytautas of Lithuania</b> at the Battle of the Vorskla River in 1399 and besieged Moscow in 1408.</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Vytautas</b> granted privileges to the Jews in 1388 and settled Tatars and Karaites near Trakai, communities whose descendants still live in Lithuania.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The Order's military reputation collapsed at the Battle of Grunwald (Tannenberg) on 15 July 1410, when a Polish-Lithuanian army under Władysław II Jagiełło and <b>Vytautas</b> killed Grand Master Ulrich von Jungingen and much of the Order's leadership.</blockquote><a href="/empire/state-of-the-teutonic-order">State of the Teutonic Order · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>On an islet of the Neman River, <b>Vytautas the Great</b>, ruler of Lithuania, and Grand Master Konrad von Jungingen signed the Treaty of Salynas, promising Samogitia to the Teutonic Knights for the third time.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-12">12 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1393 <b>Vytautas</b> expelled Fyodor Koriatovich and divided the duchy, with the western part passing to Poland.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-podolia">Duchy of Podolia</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1395 <b>Vytautas</b> removed Volodymyr Olgerdovich from the Principality of Kiev and gave him Slutsk and Kapyl in compensation.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-slutsk">Duchy of Slutsk</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Senieji Trakai, in what is now <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vilnius">Vilnius</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/lithuania">Lithuania</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=54.62,24.97&amp;year=1402" aria-label="Senieji Trakai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Vytautas the Great"><image href="/maps/globes/g030n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4751 5450h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4751 5450h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=54.62,24.97&amp;year=1402">What was here in 1402? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
